commit 2027b9321bd9b58083363b8259cb4b955743069e
Author: Martin Oberzalek <martin.oberzalek@anton-paar.com>
Date:   Thu Sep 24 22:00:12 2026 +0200

    Identify the codec: LZO1X-999 at level 8, not lzo1x_1
    
    Finding 27 left one question open - the era encoder's streams are reproducible, so
    which encoder produces them - and the answer is the high-compression variant of the
    same codec at a specific level. The proof is byte-level on shipping archives:
    
      1. rfaPack.orig.exe -Compress reproduces the shipping payloads byte for byte
         (618/618 entries of menu.rfa, 251/251 of the FH Pavlov archive), so one
         deterministic encoder made them all.
      2. It is not lzo1x_1: for 200 bytes of "ab" the era encoder emits 10 bytes
         (13 61 62 20 a5 04 00 11 00 00) where miniLZO emits 29.
      3. Of every candidate the full LZO 2.10 library offers, only lzo1x_999 produces
         those 10 bytes.
      4. Reproduced in the archive's own layout - independent 32 KiB chunks, concatenated
         - its level-8 output for menu/AirControlsPage1 is SHA-256 identical to the
         payload in the shipping archive. Six more entries agree, 60 B to 20 049 B,
         single- and multi-chunk. Level 9 is two bytes smaller and level 7 larger, which
         is what pins the level at 8 rather than "some 999".
    
    tools/lzo_variant/ keeps the probe (probe.c, chunked.c, dump_payload.py) and a README
    with the reproduction recipe, because the finding is worth nothing without the recipe.
    Its measured vendoring footprint is five objects, and decompression can stay with the
    miniLZO already vendored and tested.
    
    With this, -Compress should become byte-identical to the oracle AND readable by the
    old tools, closing findings 17 and 27 together, and the 8-26% size penalty goes away.
    Costs are recorded too: 999 is much slower per byte and needs ~448 KB of work memory
    per thread instead of ~16 KB.

commit 10182602b05cbfffad97a6fba1b592ac5c2dea20
Author: Martin Oberzalek <martin.oberzalek@anton-paar.com>
Date:   Thu Sep 24 21:43:10 2026 +0200

    Narrow finding 27: the engine reads our LZO, the 2003 tools do not
    
    Three measurements, all on shipping archives rather than fixtures.
    
    1. The game accepts our -Compress output. Our pack of the vendor menu tree was
       installed as Mods\bf1942\Archives\menu.rfa and BF1942 comes up to its main menu.
       The shipping menu.rfa is itself version 1 with 618 chunked entries, so the engine
       decodes an LZO stream every launch - and it decodes ours.
    
    2. The old tools' failure is data-dependent, not total. On that same archive our
       reader round-trips 618/618 files, while bin\rfaUnpack.orig.exe extracts 11 files
       correctly and then stops with "ERROR! Extract() fileSize: 50942 should be: 51538".
       The earlier wording ("does not accept the match opcodes miniLZO emits") came from
       probes whose files were too small to show the granularity.
    
    3. The era compressor is reproducible and is not lzo1x_1. Comparing each shipping
       archive with the vendor tool's re-pack of its extracted tree, payload by payload
       rather than by offset: 618/618 and 251/251 payloads are byte-identical, with zero
       size differences. DICE's packer and RFA Pack 1.7 share one encoder; only the
       container differs (entry order, data block placement, reserved1, flags). That is
       also why the re-packs hit the shipping size exactly.
    
       So the size penalty is purely algorithmic: 200 bytes of "ab" cost 10 bytes from the
       era encoder and 29 from miniLZO 2.10, and our menu pack is 26% larger. Which LZO
       variant that is stays an open question - there is no LZO library on this machine to
       test with, and vendoring a second codec is a D4 decision, not a probe.
    
    If it turns out to be LZO1X-999, compress mode becomes byte-identical AND readable by
    the old tools, closing findings 17 and 27 together.

commit ba133b9d51fda7691d0572b2b236b822b51cfcb0
Author: Martin Oberzalek <martin.oberzalek@anton-paar.com>
Date:   Thu Sep 24 21:34:20 2026 +0200

    A chunk can be LZO1X even when its sizes are equal
    
    Found by round-tripping the shipping Battle_of_Britain.rfa (548 entries, 46.6 MB)
    instead of a fixture. Our extraction produced 548 files and one of them was wrong;
    the vendor tool's extraction of the same archive was right:
    
        Bf1942/Levels/Battle_of_Britain/Objects/Willy/Willy.con   stored=46  unc=30
          chunk 0 : compressed=30  uncompressed=30
    
    The payload is an LZO1X stream (1e .. 11 00 00) that compresses to exactly the
    length of the file it produces, so Chunk::is_compressed() said "verbatim" and
    PayloadReader handed the compressed bytes back as content - silently, because the
    length was right. No error, no size check, nothing structural to notice.
    
    Our reader now lets the codec decide when the sizes are equal: try LZO, copy
    verbatim when it refuses. That direction is safe, because lzo1x_decompress_safe
    rejects non-streams and demands exactly the expected output length - a false
    positive would require file content to be a valid LZO1X stream of its own.
    
    This retires the "iff compressedSize != uncompressedSize" claim in AGENTS.md and
    in the repo memory: inequality still means compressed, but equality proves
    nothing. The sizes are a hint, the codec is the verdict.
    
    Verified by re-extracting the same archive: 548 of 548 files now identical to the
    vendor tool's. test_rfa_archive's
    archive_reads_a_chunk_that_compresses_to_its_own_length builds a one-entry v1
    archive around the measured 30 bytes, so the trap is pinned without the 46 MB
    fixture. 88 testcases, all green.
    
    PLAN_rfa_tools.md gains finding 30 and the phase 3 part 2 record: the two real
    archives we round-tripped and installed (both store packs byte-identical to the
    vendor tool's, both read by the game), the timings - 1.5x faster unpack, 1.8x
    slower store pack, 21x faster compress - and the note that our LZO output is
    8-14% larger and unreadable by the 2003 decoder (finding 27), so store mode stays
    the interchangeable one.

