    Add the rfaPack command line, and sort names the way the original does
    
    rfaPack (store mode) reproduces bin\rfaPack.orig.exe byte-for-byte, but the
    five-file golden tree hid a rule that the real menu.rfa exposed: the original
    compares sibling names in byte order after folding to UPPERCASE, not lowercase
    and not as raw bytes. A byte comparison put 250 of 618 entry indices in a
    different order (1,003,071 differing bytes); folding to lowercase still left 68,
    and the single remaining pair was
    
        loadingfull_256x16.dds   /   loading_full_256x16.dds
    
    '_' is 0x5F - above 'A' (0x41) and below 'a' (0x61) - so only the direction of
    the fold separates the two candidates: folding up puts loadingfull first, which
    is what the original does. Repacking work\menu_test\orig\menu (618 files from
    the vendor archive) now yields the vendor tool's own SHA-256,
    AB3C2B9E72080914..., byte for byte at 22,950,118 bytes.
    
    - rfa/RfaWriter.cc: name_less() folds to uppercase (ASCII only, so the order
      cannot depend on the machine's locale) with a documented byte-comparison
      fallback for names that fold together.
    - tests/data/golden: the tree now holds InGame/Infantry..., Icon_*/icon_* and
      loading_full/loadingfull siblings, so the archive the oracle itself wrote pins
      the rule; a testcase asserts the same order without the oracle. This is the
      fourth time a fixture too small to contain the ambiguity could not test the
      rule (findings 1, 15, 16, 28).
    - cli/PackCli.cc, src_rfaPack: the rfaPack command line. Store mode is
      byte-identical; -Compress warns on stderr about finding 27; -u is recognised
      and refused until D6-D8 are decided.
    - tools/probe_case_ties.py: scans an archive tree for case-insensitive siblings.
    - PLAN_rfa_tools.md: findings 27 and 28, and the Phase 2 store-mode gate no
      longer carries the "case-unambiguous trees only" caveat.

commit f1814c02a5867c342ae5ca725d31ab6af16787d0
Author: Martin Oberzalek <martin.oberzalek@anton-paar.com>
Date:   Thu Sep 24 20:40:28 2026 +0200

    Fail loudly from main, and add the missing error boundaries
    
    Every main() now reports to std::cerr and returns non-zero on failure. The
    three monitor tools used to fall out of main() with 0 even after printing
    an error, so a caller could not tell a failure from a success.
    
    adjust_monitor_settings is the one that needs care: its system("PAUSE")
    exists so that a double-clicked window stays open, and it is most needed
    when something went wrong. The failure is therefore recorded in a status
    that the return uses, instead of returning from the catch, so the pause
    still runs on both paths.
    
    rfaUnpack and the test runner had no boundary at all. For rfaUnpack that
    was a real defect: ToolLog::Session throws when the log file cannot be
    opened, and an escaping exception reached std::terminate with no message.
    The test runner's registration moved into make_test_cases() so that main()
    is nothing but the boundary, which also keeps the registration block at
    its original indentation instead of reindenting 90 lines.
    
    Exit codes are now 0 for success and 1 for a usage, argument or I/O
    failure. rfaUnpack keeps exiting 0 for a selection miss, because the
    oracle's golden file pins that behaviour.
    
    test_rfa.exe -t 99 still exits 1, so the suite can still report failure.
    79 testcases, all green.

